SUMMARY=[heading('Summary'),paragraph('If f is continuous and f(a),f(b) have opposite signs, bisection approximates a root of f(x)=0 in [a,b]. Its approximation is always the midpoint.'),paragraph('Under the same continuity/sign conditions, regula-falsi approximates a root in [a,b]; the approximate root will not be the midpoint but somewhere in the interval.'),paragraph('If f and its derivatives are continuous and x₀ is the initial iterate, Newton-Raphson:'),eq(r'x_{i+1}=x_i-\frac{f(x_i)}{f′(x_i)},\quad i=0,1,2,3,\ldots'),paragraph('produces successive iterates converging faster than bisection and regula-falsi.'),paragraph('If f is continuous on [a,b], the triangle rule:'),eq(r'R_n=f(x_1)\Delta x+f(x_2)\Delta x+\ldots+f(x_n)\Delta x'),paragraph('approximates ∫ₐᵇf(x)dx.'),paragraph('If f is continuous on [a,b], the trapezoidal rule is:'),eq(r'T_n=\frac{\Delta x}2[f_0+2f_1+2f_2+\ldots+2f_{n-1}+f_n],\quad\Delta x=\frac{b-a}n'),paragraph('If f is continuous on [a,b], Simpson’s rule is:'),eq(r'S_{2n}=\frac{\Delta x}3[f_0+4(f_1+f_3+\ldots+f_{2n-1})+2(f_2+f_4+\ldots+f_{2n-2})+f_{2n}],\quad\Delta x=\frac{b-a}{2n}'),note('The source “triangle rule” formula is a right-endpoint rectangle sum. Regula-falsi can coincide with the midpoint, for example when endpoint residuals have equal magnitudes and opposite signs. Newton convergence and speed require additional conditions and nonzero derivative denominators; continuity alone does not ensure them. Simpson uses 2n subintervals/n panels and its odd/even weights are distinct. Signed quadrature sums are geometric areas only for nonnegative integrands.')]
HISTORY=[heading('History'),figure('numerical325-runge','Carl David Telme Runge (1710–1761) — source caption','Source portrait caption gives Carl David Telme Runge and dates 1710–1761; retained without independently certifying those dates.'),paragraph('Carl Runge was a German mathematician and physicist. He was co-developer of the runge–kutta method in numerical analysis. He received his Ph.D degree in mathematics at Berlin in 1880. In 1886 he became professor in Hanover, Germany. He also studied spectral lines of various elements and was very interested in applying this work to astronomical spectroscopy. Runge reached retirement in 1923 but continued running his institute until his successor Gustave Herglotz arrived in 1925.'),note('This passage and portrait caption are printed source text, not independently verified history. The portrait’s 1710–1761 dates repeat the preceding Thomas Simpson portrait dates and conflict with the nineteenth/twentieth-century events in the accompanying Runge paragraph; they are not certified as accurate.')]
